* ============================================================================
* cnx_realtime_buffer_proof.dts
*
* Task #29. Does realtime CNX maintenance stay correct under TABLE BUFFER?
*
* WHY THIS EXISTS
*   CnxBackend::upsert places a record by reading its LIVE field value and
*   binary-searching the permutation against the live values of other records
*   (cnx_backend.cpp, "HOW A KEYLESS INDEX IS MAINTAINED"). That is correct when
*   the record has already been written. Under TABLE BUFFER the edit may still be
*   in the buffer, and then the probe reads the OLD value.
*
*   Two failure shapes are possible and this proof separates them:
*
*     LAG    -- the index is not maintained until COMMIT. Defensible: the order
*               simply trails the buffer, and COMMIT reconciles it.
*     WRONG  -- the index IS maintained, from the wrong value or at the wrong
*               time. This is the dangerous one, because it reports success.
*
*   The worst case is ROLLBACK. If upsert fires eagerly on a buffered edit, the
*   rollback discards the RECORD change while the INDEX keeps the move, and the
*   order then points at a value no record holds. Nothing warns: the write
*   succeeded and the index reported maintenance. B5/B6 exist for that case.
*
* NOT YET A PASS/FAIL GATE
*   B1 is OBSERVATIONAL. Both .T. and .F. are legitimate engine designs and this
*   script does not presume which was intended -- it records which world we are
*   in so the owner can rule. B2-B6 ARE load-bearing: whatever B1 shows, the
*   committed order must be correct and must agree with a REBUILD, and a rolled
*   back edit must leave no trace in the order.
*
* MARKER DESIGN
*   Inherited from CNXLIVE/IDXSTALE and load-bearing for the same reason: every
*   marker is a FIELD comparison. RECNO() and FOUND() render EMPTY in a "?"
*   marker and STR() does not rescue them. Park the cursor on a known different
*   record before any seek, then assert the field value moved.
*
* Self-contained: builds and erases its own throwaway v32 table; never touches
* STUDENTS or any live fixture.
*   Standalone:  DOTSCRIPT scripts/cnx_realtime_buffer_proof.dts
*   Capture:     SET ALTERNATE TO <file> / SET ALTERNATE ON  (never DOTSCRIPT OUT)
*
* Runtime status: NOT YET RUN. Authored 2026-08-01 against XIDX-TXN-02 M1.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* x32 lane: CNX is the v32 indexing backend
DO x32

FORMULA "CNX-BUFFER-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- fixture: throwaway v32 table, CNX order on LNAME -------------------------
ERASE TABLE CNXBUF CONFIRM
CREATE CNXBUF (SID N(8), LNAME C(20))

APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "MILLER"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 2
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ANDERSON"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 3
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ZEBRA"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 4
REPLACE LNAME WITH "YOUNG"

CNX CREATE
CNX ADDTAG LNAME
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG LNAME

* --- G0/G1: fixture guards, unbuffered --------------------------------------
* If either fails, everything below is meaningless.
GO 4
SEEK "MILLER"
? "B_G0_seek_works_before_buffer:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "MILLER")

GO TOP
? "B_G1_baseline_top_is_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- enable buffering --------------------------------------------------------
* If TABLE BUFFER refuses a v32/MSDOS table, that is itself the finding: the
* buffered path would then be unreachable for CNX and B1-B6 are vacuous.
TABLE BUFFER ON PERSISTENT

* ============================================================================
* PART 1 -- COMMIT must leave a CORRECT order
* ============================================================================

* Move MILLER (rec 1, sorts 3rd) to AAAAA (sorts 1st). Buffered.
GO 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "AAAAA"

* --- B1: OBSERVATIONAL -- is the order maintained BEFORE commit? -------------
* .T. means the order already reflects the buffered edit (eager maintenance).
* .F. means it does not (lag until commit).
* NEITHER is scored. This records which design is in force.
GO TOP
? "B1_OBSERVE_order_moved_before_commit:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

COMMIT

* --- B2: the committed order must be correct ---------------------------------
* Load-bearing regardless of B1. AAAAA sorts first, so TOP must be AAAAA. If
* upsert placed by the stale buffered value, this is where it shows.
GO TOP
? "B2_after_commit_top_is_AAAAA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- B3: the record that did not move is still correctly placed --------------
GO TOP
SKIP
? "B3_after_commit_second_is_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- B4: the tail survived ---------------------------------------------------
BOTTOM
? "B4_after_commit_bottom_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- B4b: DISCRIMINATOR -- was the FILE wrong, or only the BINDING? ---------
* B2/B3 read the order straight after COMMIT and got the PRE-edit permutation.
* B5 below reads it after REBUILD *and* SET ORDER, and gets the correct one.
* Two very different faults produce that:
*
*   .T. here -> the index FILE was correct after COMMIT's rebuild; only the
*               ACTIVE ORDER was still bound to the stale in-memory
*               permutation. Re-issuing SET ORDER fixes it with no rebuild.
*               COMMIT owes a rebind, not a rebuild.
*   .F. here -> COMMIT's rebuild genuinely produced a wrong index file, and
*               only a SECOND rebuild corrects it. Much worse.
*
* No REBUILD on this line, deliberately. That is the whole point.
SET ORDER TAG LNAME
GO TOP
? "B4b_reorder_without_rebuild_top_is_AAAAA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- B5: realtime-under-buffer must AGREE WITH REBUILD -----------------------
* The strongest assertion here. If buffering made realtime maintenance place by
* a stale value, a REBUILD will disagree and the order will CHANGE across this
* line. Same shape as CNXLIVE L_T6, but with a buffered edit behind it.
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG LNAME
GO TOP
? "B5_rebuild_agrees_with_buffered_realtime:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* ============================================================================
* PART 2 -- ROLLBACK must leave NO trace in the order
* ============================================================================
* The dangerous case. Move ZEBRA (rec 3, sorts LAST) to BBBBB (sorts 2nd), then
* discard it. If the index kept the move, rec 3 is no longer at the bottom and
* the order points at a value no record holds -- silent divergence, no warning.

GO 3
REPLACE LNAME WITH "BBBBB"
ROLLBACK

* --- B6a: the RECORD was restored -------------------------------------------
GO 3
? "B6a_rollback_restored_record:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- B6b: the ORDER was not corrupted ---------------------------------------
* ZEBRA sorts last of {AAAAA, ANDERSON, YOUNG, ZEBRA}. If the discarded edit
* leaked into the index, rec 3 moved to 2nd and BOTTOM becomes YOUNG.
BOTTOM
? "B6b_rollback_left_order_intact:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- B7: and a REBUILD still agrees -----------------------------------------
* Catches an index that survived the rollback looking plausible but differing
* from ground truth.
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG LNAME
BOTTOM
? "B7_rebuild_agrees_after_rollback:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

FORMULA "CNX-BUFFER-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown ----------------------------------------------------------------
TABLE BUFFER OFF
CLOSE
ERASE TABLE CNXBUF CONFIRM
